---
import { CATALOGO } from '../../api.js';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
// Esta pagina existe porque la API la promete: cada respuesta dice "recomputa el sha256
// segun /api-docs". Una API que apunta a una pagina que no existe pide confianza en vez
// de darla — justo lo contrario de lo que este archivo vende.
const SITE = 'https://rosettaquantum.com';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="API & MCP — Rosetta Q"
  description="Read-only API and MCP server over the Evidence Ledger. No key required. Every response carries the sealed hash and two independent public copies."
  lang="en"
  altUrl="/api-docs">
  <article class="article wrap">
    <div class="kicker">For machines · no key required</div>
    <h1>Query the evidence, don't trust the summary</h1>

    <div class="tldr">
      Every sealed run, verdict and pre-registration in the Evidence Ledger is readable
      over a public, read-only API and an MCP server. Every response carries the sealed
      <code>sha256</code> and the raw URLs of two independent public copies, so anything
      you cite can be checked rather than believed.
    </div>

    <div class="body">
      <h2>Start here</h2>
      <pre><code>curl https://rosettaquantum.com/v1/state</code></pre>
      <p>
        Returns the measured state of the archive. Today it opens with
        <strong>0 measured quantum wins</strong> — in no sealed run so far has a quantum
        method beaten the classical champion. That negative is the product of this
        archive, not a gap in it, so the API states it first.
      </p>

      <h2>Endpoints</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>What it returns</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {CATALOGO.map((e) => (<tr><td><code>GET {e.ruta}</code></td><td set:html={e.resumen + ((e.params||[]).length ? " · " + e.params.map(([n,d]) => `<code>?${n}=</code> ${d}`).join(", ") : "")} /></tr>))}
          <tr><td><code>POST /mcp</code></td><td>MCP server (JSON-RPC 2.0) for agents</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>No authentication. CORS open. Cached 5 minutes at the edge.</p>

      <h2>How to verify anything this API tells you</h2>
      <p>
        Every item carries <code>content_hash</code>, <code>github_raw</code> and
        <code>codeberg_raw</code>. The seal is computed over the file's own content:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`content_hash = sha256( json.dumps({"meta": meta_without_content_hash, <body>},
                                  sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False) )`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        where <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> is every top-level key except <code>meta</code>
        and <code>storage</code>. <code>storage</code> is excluded on purpose: it says
        where a copy lives, not what it says. Two conventions exist under the
        <code>v1</code> tag for a documented historical reason — the verifier declares
        which one each file used rather than hiding it. Full spec and the reference
        verifier are in the
        <a href="https://github.com/RosettaQuantum/evidence/blob/main/PROTOCOL.md">archive protocol</a>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`# fetch, recompute, compare — and check the two copies match each other
F=$(curl -s ${SITE}/v1/archive/V-0012)
echo "$F" | jq -r .content_hash
diff <(curl -s "$(echo "$F" | jq -r .github_raw)") \\
     <(curl -s "$(echo "$F" | jq -r .codeberg_raw)") && echo IDENTICAL`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Each sealed file is also timestamp-anchored in Bitcoin via OpenTimestamps, on a
        chain none of the parties to this archive controls.
      </p>

      <h2>MCP server</h2>
      <p>
        <code>POST {SITE}/mcp</code> speaks JSON-RPC 2.0 and exposes four read-only
        tools: <code>estado_del_archivo</code>, <code>buscar_evidencia</code>,
        <code>ver_archivo</code> and <code>listar_por_tipo</code>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`curl -X POST ${SITE}/mcp -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/list"}'`}</code></pre>

      <h2>Licence and citation</h2>
      <p>
        Data is <strong>CC BY 4.0</strong>; attribution is deliberate. Cite as:
        <em>Rosetta Quantum Evidence Ledger, CC BY 4.0 — {SITE}/ledger</em>.
        The code is Apache-2.0.
      </p>

      <h2>What this API does not do</h2>
      <p>
        It is read-only. There is no write endpoint, no <code>qlib.solve()</code>, and no
        paid tier — those arrive when there is demand and measured advantage to sell, not
        before. If you find an error in the archive,
        <a href="https://github.com/RosettaQuantum/evidence/issues">open an issue</a>:
        external correction is the QA loop this project wants.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
